[[{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/open5gs/open5gs/issues/comments/5011890717","html_url":"https://github.com/open5gs/open5gs/issues/4682#issuecomment-5011890717","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/open5gs/open5gs/issues/4682","id":5011890717,"node_id":"IC_kwDOBM4HkM8AAAABKrtiHQ","user":{"login":"fkamanzi","id":67708401,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY3NzA4NDAx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/67708401?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fkamanzi","html_url":"https://github.com/fkamanzi","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fkamanzi/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fkamanzi/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fkamanzi/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fkamanzi/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fkamanzi/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fkamanzi/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fkamanzi/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fkamanzi/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fkamanzi/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-07-18T15:51:51Z","updated_at":"2026-07-18T15:51:51Z","body":"Confirming the bug, with the spec basis and why the wrong code actually matters operationally.\n\nThe status code is wrong under both HTTP and 3GPP semantics. 500 means the responding entity\nitself hit an internal error. Here the SCP is healthy — it simply couldn't reach the upstream\ntarget NF. That's a gateway condition, and HTTP reserves distinct codes for it:\n\n- 503 Service Unavailable — target NF is down / refusing connections (your case). Ideally with\n  a Retry-After.\n- 504 Gateway Timeout — SCP reached the target but got no response in time.\n- 502 Bad Gateway — SCP got an invalid/broken response from the target.\n\nConnection-refused (NSSF stopped, port not listening) maps most naturally to 503. TS 29.500\nbacks this: the SCP in indirect communication is an HTTP proxy, and an upstream reachability\nfailure should surface as a gateway/availability status carrying a ProblemDetails cause that\nidentifies the target-NF unreachability (along the lines of TARGET_NF_NOT_REACHABLE — take the\nexact cause value from the current TS 29.500 error tables), not a generic 500.\n\nWhy this isn't cosmetic. The 500 actively breaks the SBI resilience model:\n\n- A consumer (AMF/SMF) that receives 500 from the SCP reads it as \"the SCP itself is broken\" —\n  which can trigger unnecessary SCP failover or mark the SCP unhealthy, even though the SCP is\n  fine and only the target NF is down.\n- A correct 503/504 tells the consumer the target is unavailable, which is exactly the trigger\n  for NF reselection — retry, re-discover an alternative NF instance via the NRF, apply backoff.\n  That is the whole point of delegated discovery and the SCP reselection model.\n\nSo masking a downstream outage as 500 defeats the reselection logic that indirect communication\nexists to enable. In a production MOCN mesh a target NF being down is a normal condition\n(maintenance, scaling, failover) — the SCP must classify it as such rather than reporting its\nown failure.\n\nWhere it lives. The {\"title\":\"response_handler() failed\",\"status\":500} path is the SCP\nblanketing a client-side (SCP→target) failure into a generic 500. The fix is to distinguish, in\nthat handler, a connection-level failure (ConnectionRefused / no response / timeout) from a\ngenuine internal error, and emit the corresponding 502/503/504 + ProblemDetails cause instead of\n500. The connection-refused case in your repro should become a 503.\n","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/open5gs/open5gs/issues/comments/5011890717/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_